data-toggle="false" data-gravity="n"><svg viewBox="0 0 1000 1000" class="icon" height="1em" width="1em"><path d="m250 850l-187 0-63 0 0-62 0-188 63 0 0 188 187 0 0 62z m688 0l-188 0 0-62 188 0 0-188 62 0 0 188 0 62-62 0z m-875-938l0 188-63 0 0-188 0-62 63 0 187 0 0 62-187 0z m875 188l0-188-188 0 0-62 188 0 62 0 0 62 0 188-62 0z m-125 188l-1 0-93-94-156 156 156 156 92-93 2 0 0 250-250 0 0-2 93-92-156-156-156 156 94 92 0 2-250 0 0-250 0 0 93 93 157-156-157-156-93 94 0 0 0-250 250 0 0 0-94 93 156 157 156-157-93-93 0 0 250 0 0 250z" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 850)"></path></svg></a></div></div></div><svg class="main-svg" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="700" height="405"><g class="hoverlayer"></g></svg></div></div></div></foreignObject></g><g x="360" y="270" transform="translate(360,270)" width="25" height="15" class="part" name="part" xo="0" yo="0" sref="G4" directory="standard" liblocale="true" symbolfile="GND" model="GND"><g width="150" top="572" left="0" height="150" zoom="20" reference="G" std="true" description=" " modelname="GND" setref="GND" symbolname="GND"></g><polyline points="9,12 21,12 " class="polyline" name="polyline" style="stroke: rgb(0, 0, 255); fill: none; stroke-width: 1px;"></polyline><polyline points="13,14 17,14 " class="polyline" name="polyline" style="stroke: rgb(0, 0, 255); fill: none; stroke-width: 1px;"></polyline><g points="15,5 15,10" class="polyline" name="pin" type="simple" netId="25" netIdPos="0"><polyline points="15,5 15,10 " style="stroke: rgb(255, 0, 0); stroke-width: 1px;"></polyline><rect width="6" height="6" class="pin" x="12" y="2" style="stroke: rgb(0, 255, 0); fill: rgb(255, 0, 0); stroke-width: 1px; display: none;"></rect><text r="0" x="13" y="12" transform="rotate(90 13 12)" style="font-size: 12px; font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;; display: none;">p</text><text r="0" x="15" y="5" transform="rotate(0 15 5)" style="font-size: 12px; font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;; display: none;"> </text><ellipse cx="15" cy="8.5" rx="1.5" ry="1.5" style="stroke: rgb(255, 0, 0); fill: rgb(255, 0, 0); stroke-width: 1px; display: none;"></ellipse><polygon points="13.5,10 16.5,10 15,11.5 " style="stroke: rgb(255, 0, 0); fill: rgb(255, 0, 0); stroke-width: 1px; display: none;"></polygon></g><polyline points="7,10 23,10 " class="polyline" name="polyline" style="stroke: rgb(0, 0, 255); fill: none; stroke-width: 1px;"></polyline></g><polyline points="375,275 375,260 375,260 " class="polyline" name="net" xdir="true" ref="0" parent="false" parentcolor="false" used="true" id="25" node0="-1" node1="7" style="stroke: rgb(0, 0, 0); fill: none; stroke-width: 1px;"></polyline><text class="draggable" name="text" x="212" y="177" r="0" rtemp="0" transform="rotate(0 212 177)" style="fill: rgb(185, 24, 24); font-weight: bold; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px; font-family: bold;">V(out1)</text><text class="draggable" name="text" x="377" y="175" r="0" rtemp="0" transform="rotate(0 377 175)" style="fill: rgb(50, 50, 200); font-weight: bold; font-style: normal; font-size: 8px; font-family: bold;">V(out2)</text><text class="draggable" name="text" x="546" y="175" r="0" rtemp="0" transform="rotate(0 546 175)" style="fill: rgb(54, 179, 181); font-weight: bold; font-style: normal; font-size: 8px; font-family: bold;">V(out3)</text><g transform="translate(677,18)" x="677" y="18" title="codeHTML" width="514" height="694" class="codeHTML" name="codeHTML" rot="0"><foreignObject x="0" y="0" width="222" height="200" style="height: 694px; width: 856.667px;"><div name="htmlCode" style="zoom: 60%; height: 694px; width: 856.667px;" code="<div class=&quot;ch&quot;>
    
    <h1>3-Stage CMOS Ring Oscillator with RC Phase Shift - SPICE Time Domain Analysis</h1>

    <div class=&quot;ch-card&quot;>
        <h2>Circuit Components</h2>
        <h3 style=&quot;color: #2980b9;&quot;>Three CMOS Inverter Stages</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Stage 1:</strong> M1 (PMOS) + M2 (NMOS) → Output V(out1)</li>
            <li><strong>Stage 2:</strong> M3 (PMOS) + M4 (NMOS) → Output V(out2)</li>
            <li><strong>Stage 3:</strong> M5 (PMOS) + M6 (NMOS) → Output V(out3)</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 style=&quot;color: #c0392b; margin-top: 15px;&quot;>RC Phase-Shift Networks</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>R1, R2, R3:</strong> 100 Ω each (series resistors)</li>
            <li><strong>C1, C2, C3:</strong> 50 μF each (shunt capacitors to ground)</li>
            <li><strong>Vcc:</strong> 10V DC power supply</li>
            <li><strong>Analysis:</strong> Time Domain (0s to 1.5s)</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Oscillation Principle (Barkhausen Criterion)</h2>
    <div class=&quot;ch-formula&quot;>
        Total Phase Shift = 3 × (180° inverter + 60° RC network) = 3 × 240° = 720° ≡ 0° (mod 360°)<br>
        Each RC stage provides ≈ 60° phase shift at oscillation frequency<br>
        f ≈ 1 / (2π·R·C·√6) ≈ 1 / (2π × 100 × 50μ × 2.45) ≈ <strong>13 Hz</strong> (approximate)<br>
        Observed period ≈ 0.2s → f ≈ <strong>5 Hz</strong> (due to MOSFET delays)
    </div>

    <h2>Simulation Results</h2>
    <div class=&quot;ch-grid&quot;>

        <div class=&quot;ch-col blue&quot;>
            <h3>Startup Phase (0 to ~0.75s)</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>All three outputs settle at ~4.5-5V (metastable point)</li>
                <li>Circuit is in unstable equilibrium (Vout ≈ Vcc/2)</li>
                <li>Small noise/imbalance triggers oscillation</li>
                <li>Gradual growth of oscillation amplitude</li>
                <li>Positive feedback builds up the signal</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class=&quot;ch-col red&quot;>
            <h3>Steady-State Oscillation (~0.8s to 1.5s)</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Three sinusoidal waveforms with 120° phase shift</li>
                <li>Amplitude: ~1V to ~9V (near rail-to-rail)</li>
                <li>Frequency: ~5 Hz (period ≈ 0.2s)</li>
                <li>V(out1), V(out2), V(out3) are equally spaced in phase</li>
                <li>Stable sustained oscillation achieved</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

    </div>

    <div class=&quot;ch-card green-border&quot;>
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p style=&quot;line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;&quot;>This circuit is a <strong>3-Stage CMOS Ring Oscillator</strong> with RC phase-shift networks between stages. Each CMOS inverter provides 180° phase inversion, while each RC network adds approximately 60° of additional phase shift, giving a total of 240° per stage. With three stages, the total phase shift is 720° (equivalent to 0°), satisfying the <strong>Barkhausen criterion</strong> for oscillation. The circuit initially settles at a metastable point (Vout ≈ Vcc/2) where all inverters are in their linear region. Small perturbations (noise, mismatch) cause the circuit to break away from this unstable equilibrium, and the oscillation amplitude grows until it reaches steady-state. The three outputs are phase-shifted by 120° from each other, making this circuit useful for generating <strong>three-phase signals</strong>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class=&quot;ch-card&quot; style=&quot;background-color: #e8f8f5; border-left: 4px solid #1abc9c;&quot;>
        <h2 style=&quot;color: #16a085;&quot;>Key Characteristics &amp; Applications</h2>
        <ul style=&quot;line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;&quot;>
            <li><strong>Odd Number of Stages:</strong> Required for oscillation (3, 5, 7... stages)</li>
            <li><strong>RC Networks:</strong> Control oscillation frequency and provide additional phase shift</li>
            <li><strong>Metastable Startup:</strong> Circuit needs perturbation to start oscillating</li>
            <li><strong>Three-Phase Output:</strong> 120° separation between outputs</li>
            <li><strong>Applications:</strong> Clock generation, three-phase signal generation, frequency references, PLL circuits, motor control signals</li>
            <li><strong>Frequency Tuning:</strong> Adjust R or C values to change oscillation frequency</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

</div>"><div class="ch">
    
    <h1>3-Stage CMOS Ring Oscillator with RC Phase Shift - SPICE Time Domain Analysis</h1>

    <div class="ch-card">
        <h2>Circuit Components</h2>
        <h3 style="color: #2980b9;">Three CMOS Inverter Stages</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Stage 1:</strong> M1 (PMOS) + M2 (NMOS) → Output V(out1)</li>
            <li><strong>Stage 2:</strong> M3 (PMOS) + M4 (NMOS) → Output V(out2)</li>
            <li><strong>Stage 3:</strong> M5 (PMOS) + M6 (NMOS) → Output V(out3)</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 style="color: #c0392b; margin-top: 15px;">RC Phase-Shift Networks</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>R1, R2, R3:</strong> 100 Ω each (series resistors)</li>
            <li><strong>C1, C2, C3:</strong> 50 μF each (shunt capacitors to ground)</li>
            <li><strong>Vcc:</strong> 10V DC power supply</li>
            <li><strong>Analysis:</strong> Time Domain (0s to 1.5s)</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Oscillation Principle (Barkhausen Criterion)</h2>
    <div class="ch-formula">
        Total Phase Shift = 3 × (180° inverter + 60° RC network) = 3 × 240° = 720° ≡ 0° (mod 360°)<br>
        Each RC stage provides ≈ 60° phase shift at oscillation frequency<br>
        f ≈ 1 / (2π·R·C·√6) ≈ 1 / (2π × 100 × 50μ × 2.45) ≈ <strong>13 Hz</strong> (approximate)<br>
        Observed period ≈ 0.2s → f ≈ <strong>5 Hz</strong> (due to MOSFET delays)
    </div>

    <h2>Simulation Results</h2>
    <div class="ch-grid">

        <div class="ch-col blue">
            <h3>Startup Phase (0 to ~0.75s)</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>All three outputs settle at ~4.5-5V (metastable point)</li>
                <li>Circuit is in unstable equilibrium (Vout ≈ Vcc/2)</li>
                <li>Small noise/imbalance triggers oscillation</li>
                <li>Gradual growth of oscillation amplitude</li>
                <li>Positive feedback builds up the signal</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="ch-col red">
            <h3>Steady-State Oscillation (~0.8s to 1.5s)</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Three sinusoidal waveforms with 120° phase shift</li>
                <li>Amplitude: ~1V to ~9V (near rail-to-rail)</li>
                <li>Frequency: ~5 Hz (period ≈ 0.2s)</li>
                <li>V(out1), V(out2), V(out3) are equally spaced in phase</li>
                <li>Stable sustained oscillation achieved</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

    </div>

    <div class="ch-card green-border">
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p style="line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;">This circuit is a <strong>3-Stage CMOS Ring Oscillator</strong> with RC phase-shift networks between stages. Each CMOS inverter provides 180° phase inversion, while each RC network adds approximately 60° of additional phase shift, giving a total of 240° per stage. With three stages, the total phase shift is 720° (equivalent to 0°), satisfying the <strong>Barkhausen criterion</strong> for oscillation. The circuit initially settles at a metastable point (Vout ≈ Vcc/2) where all inverters are in their linear region. Small perturbations (noise, mismatch) cause the circuit to break away from this unstable equilibrium, and the oscillation amplitude grows until it reaches steady-state. The three outputs are phase-shifted by 120° from each other, making this circuit useful for generating <strong>three-phase signals</strong>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="ch-card" style="background-color: #e8f8f5; border-left: 4px solid #1abc9c;">
        <h2 style="color: #16a085;">Key Characteristics &amp; Applications</h2>
        <ul style="line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;">
            <li><strong>Odd Number of Stages:</strong> Required for oscillation (3, 5, 7... stages)</li>
            <li><strong>RC Networks:</strong> Control oscillation frequency and provide additional phase shift</li>
            <li><strong>Metastable Startup:</strong> Circuit needs perturbation to start oscillating</li>
            <li><strong>Three-Phase Output:</strong> 120° separation between outputs</li>
            <li><strong>Applications:</strong> Clock generation, three-phase signal generation, frequency references, PLL circuits, motor control signals</li>
            <li><strong>Frequency Tuning:</strong> Adjust R or C values to change oscillation frequency</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

</div></div></foreignObject></g>